body:
Storing `origin.interface(db)` here means `ProtocolInterfaceView::base()` now returns the identity interface (`P[T]`) rather than the concrete specialization (`P[int]`). That changes the input to the existing [filtering call in `try_check_non_recursive_protocol_members`](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/blob/771b038d3a69b3117f400e5cb8241abaf8e7f34f/crates/ty_python_semantic/src/types/instance.rs#L1257-L1264): it passes `target_interface.base()` to `non_recursive_protocol_interface` before specialized member data is resolved.

In the example below, that filter sees the descriptor's identity result, `int | P[T]`, and drops the member as recursive. For `P[int]`, however, the descriptor selects an overload returning the finite type `int`. Filtering the identity interface therefore loses a requirement that the specialized interface would retain, which breaks callback inference.

On the base commit (`7857566b3d`), the example below reveals `int`; on this head (`771b038d3a`), it reveals `Unknown`.

Could we account for specialization-dependent descriptor results before filtering, or avoid this shortcut for those members?

```python
from __future__ import annotations
from typing import Callable, Protocol, overload, reveal_type

class Descriptor[T]:
    def __init__(self, getter: Callable[..., T]) -> None: ...
    @overload
    def __get__(self: Descriptor[int], instance: object, owner: type | None = None) -> int: ...
    @overload
    def __get__(self, instance: object, owner: type | None = None) -> int | P[T]: ...
    def __get__(self, instance: object, owner: type | None = None) -> int | P[T]:
        raise NotImplementedError

class P[T](Protocol):
    @Descriptor
    def value(self) -> T: ...

def consume(value: P[int]) -> None: ...
def infer[T](callback: Callable[[P[T]], None]) -> T:
    raise NotImplementedError

reveal_type(infer(consume))
```

