This is just newly exposing a pre-existing issue, so maybe it should just be a follow-up, but removing this projection exposes a pre-existing problem in [return-context preference extraction](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/blob/2f9e80196f363c95d98c971cf244520c897a8aaa/crates/ty_python_semantic/src/types/call/bind.rs#L6108-L6141). A reproducer is:

```py
def inner[T](value: T) -> list[T] | T | None:
    raise NotImplementedError

def outer[T](value: T) -> list[T] | T | None:
    return inner(value)
```

This now reports `invalid-return-type`, with the call returning `list[T | list[T] | None] | T | list[T] | None` instead of `list[T] | T | None`.

Calling the inner and outer type variables `I` and `O`, the return-context comparison retains both an invariant `I = O` path and a covariant, upper-only `I ≤ list[O] | O | None` path, with a residual `list[I] ≤ O` condition. `variance_map` joins their variances into invariant, so the upper-only candidate incorrectly passes the covariance filter. Its `None` arm also lets it pass the concrete-content filter, and the wider type is then seeded as an exact preference. The old projection collapses this comparison to the upper-only path, which the covariance filter skips.

Applying the covariance filter within `solve_with` fixes both this example and the `Sequence` forwarding example. This change passes all tests, plus a control where an invariant `list[T | None]` context must still widen the inferred type::

```diff
--- a/crates/ty_python_semantic/src/types/call/bind.rs
+++ b/crates/ty_python_semantic/src/types/call/bind.rs
@@ -6105,17 +6105,26 @@
